{"format":"vetro-plugin","version":1,"manifest":{"id":"notification-center","name":"Notification Center","version":"0.6.1","apiVersion":1,"permissions":["exec"],"author":"mclaudiopt","description":"Native TV banners for notifications from any LAN device, with optional sounds. Runs one fixed paplay command; fetches a sender's image URL only if sent.","main":"index.js","ui":{"label":"Notifications"}},"files":{"API.md":"# Notification Center API\n\nEverything the Notification Center can show on the TV can be sent as an HTTP request, from **any source**: another Shard on the same TV, a PC, a phone, a script,\nHome Assistant, a NAS, a doorbell, or any app that can publish to [ntfy](https://ntfy.sh). The panel is only one more client of this API.\n\nThe same description is available from the Shard itself: `GET /plugins/notification-center/api/api` returns the fields, enums, limits and routes as JSON, built from the code that\nvalidates the requests. Use it to discover what a given TV supports.\n\n> **Rule for contributors.** A new field, mode or route is added in four places together: the code (`index.js`, plus `nt.js` / `svc` if it changes what is shown), the\n> self-description (`describe()` in `index.js`), this file, and the panel (`ui.html`). Fields are only ever added, never renamed or removed, so existing senders keep working.\n\n## Where to send\n\n| From | Address |\n|---|---|\n| Another Shard on the same TV | `http://127.0.0.1:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/<route>` (no key needed) |\n| Any device on the LAN | `http://<TV-IP>:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/<route>` |\n\nWith Vetro pairing **off** no key is needed. With pairing **on**, devices other than the TV itself send `Authorization: Bearer <key>` (pair once with `POST /auth/pair`, then\n`POST /auth/confirm` with the 6-digit code shown on the TV, as in Vetro's `SIGN-IN.md`).\n\n## Send a notification: `POST notify`\n\nJSON body. Only `message` is required.\n\n| Field | Type | Default | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `message` | string, max 200 | required | The text. |\n| `title` | string, max 40 | empty | Bold line above the message. |\n| `level` | `info` `ok` `warn` `alert` | `info` | Colours the card and the panel; the native banner gets the level's emoji. |\n| `mode` | `banner` `full` `both` | `banner` | `banner`: the native Samsung banner over whatever is running, no app switch. `full`: a full-screen card (the companion app comes to the front). `both`: card and banner. |\n| `duration` | integer 1-60 | `8` | Seconds on screen. |\n| `icon` | string, max 16 | by level | One emoji (or emoji sequence) in front of the title, e.g. the ball for a goal. Cut at whole emoji only. |\n| `image` | http/https URL, max 290 | none | A picture under the message on the **full-screen card**. |\n| `wallpaper` | http/https URL, max 290 | none | A darkened full-screen background behind the card. Can be combined with `image`. |\n| `seconds` | integer 5-300 | `30` | How long it may wait in the queue (always at least `duration` + 20). |\n| `open` | boolean | `true` | `false` only queues it, without launching the app or its service. |\n| `source` | string, max 32 (`a-z 0-9 . _ -`) | none | Who is sending (or the header `X-NC-Source`). If a source with that name exists, its defaults, key, on/off switch and rate limit apply, see \"Sources\". |\n| `group` | boolean | `true` | `false` = never merge this notification with an identical recent one (the panel sends `false` for Resend and the test buttons, so they always show). Merging only happens while the setting `groupSeconds` is above 0. |\n| `sound` | `chime` `soft` `reward` `success` `alert` `rotation` `open` `poweron` | none | Plays that TV system sound with the notification. Played by this Shard itself (one fixed `paplay` command), at most one sound every 0.8 s. It plays when the notification appears, not when it is queued: in `banner` mode it plays when the banner service has shown the banner, or `soundDelayMs` after the request (default 3 s, at most 10 s); in `full` and `both` when the app screen asks for the card (at most 10 s after the request); with `open: false` (nothing is shown) it plays at once. At most 8 sounds wait at the same time; a request over that gets `\"sound\":\"skipped\"`. Not played for a notification held back by quiet hours or merged as a repeat. |\n| `volume` | integer 10-100 | `100` | Volume of the sound, in percent. A number below 10 is raised to 10 and above 100 lowered to 100 (so `volume: 0` is the quietest, never the loudest); missing or not a number means `100`. For the default volume of a source or a webhook, `0` or an empty value clears it. |\n\nAnswer: `{\"ok\":true,\"id\":\"1-abc\",\"open\":\"requested\",\"sound\":\"none\"}` (`sound` is `\"requested\"` when a sound was asked for). Errors: `400 {\"error\":\"message is required\"}` or `\"invalid JSON\"`; `403` (`invalid key for this source`, `source is disabled`, `unknown source`); `429` (`rate limit for this source`).\nTwo other answers are still `200`: during quiet hours `{\"ok\":true,\"id\":\"...\",\"suppressed\":\"quiet hours\"}` (recorded in `GET recent`, not shown), and a repeat inside the grouping window `{\"ok\":true,\"id\":\"...\",\"merged\":true,\"count\":2}`.\n\nNotes:\n- `image` and `wallpaper` are fetched **by the Shard** (the app may only load origins it declares) and served back to the app. Up to 3 redirects, 8 s, 3 MB, png/jpeg/gif/webp/bmp only.\n  The URL may be on your LAN (a camera snapshot). A picture that cannot be fetched shows \"FAILED to load\" with its address on the card.\n- The native banner (`mode: banner`) takes plain text and a duration only: no image, no colour. Use `full` or `both` for pictures.\n- The queue holds up to 5 active notifications; the full-screen app is launched when it is not already open (it announces itself while open and when it leaves), at most once every 2 s, and a notification queued while it is open extends its stay; the banner service has no such limit.\n\n```\ncurl -X POST http://<TV-IP>:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/notify \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"title\":\"Doorbell\",\"message\":\"Someone is at the door\",\"mode\":\"full\",\"level\":\"alert\",\"duration\":20,\n       \"image\":\"http://camera.local/snapshot.jpg\",\"wallpaper\":\"https://example.com/night.jpg\"}'\n```\n\n### From another Shard (ES5, no dependencies)\n\n```js\nvar http = require('http');\nfunction notify(fields) {                       // fields: the table above\n    var body = JSON.stringify(fields);\n    var rq = http.request({ host: '127.0.0.1', port: 8099, method: 'POST', path: '/plugins/notification-center/api/notify',\n        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Content-Length': Buffer.byteLength(body) } }, function (r) { r.resume(); });\n    rq.on('error', function () { /* Notification Center not installed or Vetro busy */ });\n    rq.end(body);\n}\nnotify({ title: 'Goal', message: 'Team 1-0 Rival (23\\')', level: 'ok', duration: 30 });\n```\n\nThe Match alerts and Cast Shards use exactly this call.\n\n### Other languages\n\n```powershell\nInvoke-RestMethod -Method Post -Uri \"http://<TV-IP>:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/notify\" -ContentType \"application/json\" `\n  -Body (@{ title = \"Backup\"; message = \"Finished\"; level = \"ok\" } | ConvertTo-Json)\n```\n```python\nimport json, urllib.request\nreq = urllib.request.Request(\"http://<TV-IP>:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/notify\",\n    json.dumps({\"title\": \"Oven\", \"message\": \"Dinner is ready\", \"mode\": \"both\"}).encode(), {\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"})\nprint(urllib.request.urlopen(req).read())\n```\nHome Assistant (`configuration.yaml`):\n```yaml\nrest_command:\n  tv_notify:\n    url: \"http://<TV-IP>:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/notify\"\n    method: post\n    content_type: \"application/json\"\n    payload: '{\"title\":\"{{ title }}\",\"message\":\"{{ message }}\",\"level\":\"{{ level | default(''info'') }}\"}'\n```\n\n## ntfy-compatible: `POST ntfy`\n\nFor apps that already publish to ntfy. The body is the message; headers `Title`, `Priority` (`5` = alert, `4` = warning) and `Tags` (`warning`, `rotating_light`,\n`white_check_mark`, `tada`, `fire`, `soccer`, `bell`, `house`, `package`, `sunny`, `skull` set the icon and level). Or JSON `{\"message\",\"title\",\"priority\",\"tags\"}`.\n\n```\ncurl -X POST http://<TV-IP>:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/ntfy -H \"Title: Oven\" -H \"Priority: 4\" -H \"Tags: tada\" -d \"Dinner is ready\"\n```\n\nA sound can be chosen with the header `Sound` (or the JSON field `sound`, and `volume`), see `sound` above.\n\n## Read and discover\n\n| Route | Returns |\n|---|---|\n| `GET api` | This API as JSON: fields, enums, limits, routes, version. |\n| `GET notifications` | `{lang, notifications:[{id,title,message,level,mode,duration,icon,image,wallpaper,seconds}]}` (the active queue; `?js=1` for the script form used by the app). |\n| `GET recent` | `{recent:[{id,title,message,level,mode,duration,icon,image,wallpaper,at}]}` (the last 20 sent). |\n| `GET img?id=<id>[&kind=wallpaper][&info=1]` | The picture of a queued notification; `info=1` returns `{ok,type,bytes}` or `{ok:false,error}` (use it to check a URL). |\n| `GET status` | `{version, app, appVersion, service, handoff, pairing:\"on\"\\|\"off\"\\|\"unknown\"}`: is the companion app and its banner service installed, is Vetro pairing on, did the Shard write the queue file the banner service reads (`handoff`). |\n| `GET lang` / `POST lang` | Display language `{lang:\"en\"|\"pt\"}`, shared by the panel and the TV screen. |\n| `GET sounds` | `{sounds:[...], songs:[{name,title}], available, volume, delayMs, trace, last}`: the sounds a request can choose, the tunes of `POST play`, whether this Shard can play them (`exec` granted), the sound settings, when the last banner sound started (`trace`) and the result of the last attempt. |\n| `POST play` | `{sound, volume?}`: play one sound without a notification (answer `{ok:true,sound:\"requested\"}`, `400` for an unknown name, `429` if another sound was played less than 0.8 s ago). `{song:\"rock\"}` or `{song:\"fate\"}`: a goodie, about 10 seconds of a well-known tune (*We Will Rock You*, the start of Beethoven's 5th) made only of these sounds and recognised by the rhythm; one song at a time (`409` while one plays), `{song:\"stop\"}` stops it. |\n| `GET emoji` | The bundled emoji list (Unicode CLDR, MIT) used by the panel's icon picker. |\n\n## Sources, quiet hours, grouping and webhooks\n\n**Sources** give each sender its own defaults and rules. A source is a short name (`doorbell`, `home-assistant`, `match-alerts`); senders put it in `source` (or the header `X-NC-Source`).\n\n| Route | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `GET profiles` | `{profiles:{<source>:{enabled,icon,level,mode,duration,rate,hasKey}}, max:20}`. The key is never returned. |\n| `POST profiles` | Create, change or delete one: `{source, icon?, level?, mode?, duration?, sound?, volume?, rate?, enabled?, key?, genKey?, delete?}`. `icon`, `level`, `mode`, `duration`, `sound` and `volume` fill in what the sender leaves out (the sender's own value always wins). `rate` is the most notifications per minute (`0` = no limit). `key` (8-64 characters; `\"\"` clears it) makes the source require the header `X-NC-Key`; `genKey: true` creates a random key and returns it **once**. |\n\n**Settings:**\n\n| Route | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `GET settings` | `{settings:{quiet:{enabled,from,to,allow},groupSeconds,strict,soundVolume,soundDelayMs}}` |\n| `POST settings` | Send only what changes. `quiet`: between `from` and `to` (`\"22:00\"` to `\"07:00\"`, TV local time, may cross midnight) nothing is shown below the level `allow` (`alert` lets only alerts through, `warn` lets warnings and alerts, `none` silences everything); suppressed ones are still listed in `GET recent`. `groupSeconds` (0-3600, default 60, `0` = off): a notification identical to one from the last `groupSeconds` (same source, title, message and level) is counted (`count` in `GET recent`) instead of shown again, and the answer is `{merged:true,count}`; send `group:false` to always show one (grouping is on by default, so a repeat pressed twice within a minute shows nothing). Only the last 200 distinct notifications are remembered. `strict: true` rejects notifications whose `source` has no profile. `soundVolume` (10-100 %, default 100) is the volume of every sound (the system sounds are quiet, so 100 % is already boosted; the `volume` of a request is a share of it). `soundDelayMs` (0-10000, default 3000) is how long after the request the sound starts: the banner appears a few seconds after the request (Vetro has to start the banner service) and the Shard cannot see exactly when, so you tune this by ear in the Testing tab; if the banner service did record the id in a file the Shard can read, the sound starts as soon as it is seen. |\n\n**Webhooks** turn any JSON a service already sends (GitHub, Home Assistant, Grafana, a router) into a notification, without changing the sender:\n\n| Route | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `GET hooks` | `{hooks:{<name>:{title,message,level,icon,mode,duration,source}}, max:10, base}` |\n| `POST hooks` | Create, change or delete one: `{name, message, title?, level?, icon?, mode?, duration?, sound?, volume?, source?, delete?}`. `name`: `a-z 0-9 -`, up to 24. The text fields are templates: `{{a.b.0.c}}` is replaced by that value of the posted JSON (a missing value gives an empty string; nothing is ever evaluated). `message` is required. |\n| `POST hook/<name>` | Send any JSON (up to 64 KB) to that hook. A body that is not JSON is available as `{{text}}`. The notification is sent as source `source`, or the hook's name if `source` is empty, so a source's key protects its hook. |\n\nExample, GitHub pushes: `POST hooks` with `{\"name\":\"github\",\"title\":\"{{repository.name}}\",\"message\":\"{{pusher.name}} pushed {{head_commit.message}}\",\"icon\":\"📦\"}`, then give GitHub the URL `http://<TV-IP>:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/hook/github`.\n\n## Security notes\n\n- This Shard has the `exec` permission for exactly one command: `paplay --volume=<integer> /usr/share/feedback/sound/operation/<one of 8 fixed files>`. The sound is chosen from a closed list and the volume is a clamped integer, so no text from a request reaches the command. Anyone who can reach the Shard can make the TV play those sounds (at most one every 0.8 s); with pairing on they need a key.\n\n- A source key is stored in `ctx.config` on the TV in plain text and checked by the Shard. It stops **misconfigured** senders (a script that picks the wrong source, a service you did not mean to allow), **not hostile ones**: anyone who can reach the Shard can also read the list of sources and change or delete a profile, including its key (`POST profiles`). It travels in clear text on the LAN like the rest of the traffic. Use Vetro pairing to keep strangers out; use keys to keep your own senders apart. `GET profiles` never returns a key.\n- Anyone who can reach the Shard can change sources, settings and hooks, as they can change the language. Hooks and sources can only shape the text, icon, level, mode and duration of a notification: nothing can point the Shard at another app or run a command.\n\n- With pairing off, anyone who can reach the TV's port 8099 can send notifications and make the Shard fetch an image URL. Turn pairing on for shared networks.\n- The Shard only fetches `http`/`https` URLs, never `file:` or other schemes, and serves only image types.\n- The active queue (5) is kept in memory only. The list of the last 20 sent (`GET recent`) is kept in `ctx.config` on the TV so it survives restarts and updates; it holds the fields you sent, including any image or wallpaper URL. The display language is also kept there.\n","CHANGELOG.md":"# Changelog\n\n## 0.6.1\n- **A volume below 10 is no longer the maximum:** `volume: 0`, `5` or `-3` is raised to 10 (the quietest), above 100 is lowered to 100, and only a missing or non-numeric volume means the default 100. One rule (`clampVolume` in `rules.js`) for `notify`, `POST play`, sources, webhooks and the `soundVolume` setting (which also accepts text such as `\"5\"`).\n- At most 8 sounds wait for their banner at once (each waits with a 50 ms timer); a request over that gets `sound: \"skipped\"` instead of piling up timers.\n- The docs now say what the code does (banner mode waits `soundDelayMs`, full and both up to 10 s), and the delay slider goes up to 10000 like the setting.\nFollow-ups from the catalog review of 0.3.1 (versions 0.4.0 to 0.6.0 were only published in the author's own repository).\n- The grouping memory is bounded: nothing is stored when grouping is off, entries older than the window are dropped, and at most 200 are kept (a flood of distinct notifications no longer slows every request).\n- New field `group` (`false` = never merge): the panel's Resend and test buttons send it, so they always show instead of answering `merged` while the panel says \"Sent again\".\n- README \"Data it keeps\" lists the sources (with their keys, stored as text), settings and webhooks; API.md says that a source key stops misconfigured senders, not hostile ones.\n\n## 0.6.0\n- **Volume setting:** `soundVolume` (10-100 %, default 100) replaces the old boost (`soundGain`); a slider next to the sound selector in the send form and in the Testing tab. The `volume` of a request is a share of it.\n- **The sound is in time with the banner:** `soundDelayMs` (default 3000 ms) is how long after the request the sound starts; tune it by ear in the Testing tab (the banner service cannot tell the Shard when the banner appears). If the banner is recorded in the file the Shard reads, the sound starts as soon as it is seen. `GET sounds` has a `trace` of the last timing.\n- **Goodie** in the Testing tab (`POST play` with `song`): about 10 seconds of a well-known tune made only of the notification sounds (*We Will Rock You*, the start of Beethoven's 5th), recognised by the rhythm. One song at a time, fixed in the code, same fixed command.\n\n## 0.5.0\n- **Sounds are part of this Shard now** (they were a separate notification-sound Shard in 0.4.0, which is no longer needed). The Shard asks for the `exec` permission for exactly one command, `paplay` on a fixed list of the TV's system sounds; the sound is chosen from a closed list and the volume is a clamped integer, so nothing from a request reaches the command. At most one sound every 0.8 s.\n- The sound goes with the banner: it plays when the banner service has shown it (the Shard reads the file the service writes), when the app screen asks for a card, or after `soundDelayMs` (banner mode, default 3 s) or 10 s at the latest (full and both); with `open: false` at once.\n- `sound` and `volume` on `notify`, ntfy, webhooks and source defaults; routes `GET sounds` and `POST play`; the boost is the setting `soundGain` (100-300 %, default 200) in `POST settings`; `GET status` reports `sound`.\n- Panel: the Testing tab has the boost slider next to the sound tests.\n- Tests with a fake `ctx.execute` that records every command and checks its shape.\n\n## 0.4.0\n- **Sounds:** any app can choose one of the TV's system sounds with `sound` (and `volume`) on `notify`, ntfy, webhooks and source defaults. In 0.4.0 the sound was played by a separate notification-sound Shard (merged into this one in 0.5.0). No sound unless one is asked for, and none for a notification held back by quiet hours or merged as a repeat. Routes `GET sounds` and `POST play`; `GET status` reports `sound`.\n- Panel: new **Testing** tab (test banner/card/both, the API \"Try it\" form and sound tests); a sound selector in the send form and in the source and webhook forms.\n- Tests with a fake Vetro for all of the above (`tests/notification-center-sound.test.cjs`).\n\n## 0.3.1\nFixes from the catalog review.\n- A request can no longer make the Shard fail: every value from the network is type-checked before it is converted (`{\"message\":{\"toString\":1}}` and similar threw), every body handler and the router catch errors, names are looked up with `hasOwnProperty` (`__proto__` and `constructor` are not levels, modes or sources).\n- ntfy `tags` is only a real array or a comma list, at most 10 entries (a crafted `length` froze Vetro).\n- Pictures: the route that fetched any URL (`img?url=`) is gone, the panel preview loads the URL itself; at most 2 downloads at once, 10 s in total each, and `stop()` aborts them.\n- The queue file in `/tmp` is written under a new name (`wx`) and renamed over the target, so a symlink planted there is not written through.\n- Resend keeps the wallpaper; request bodies are decoded as UTF-8 after all chunks arrive; the manifest description says that image URLs are fetched; README corrected about launch limits.\n\n## 0.3.0\n- **Sources:** a `source` per sender (or header `X-NC-Source`) with its own defaults (icon, level, mode, duration), on/off switch, rate limit per minute and optional key (header `X-NC-Key`, generated by the Shard, never returned). Routes `GET`/`POST profiles`.\n- **Quiet hours:** a time window (may cross midnight) in which notifications below a chosen level are only recorded, not shown. **Grouping:** identical repeats inside a window are counted instead of shown again. Strict mode rejects unknown sources. Routes `GET`/`POST settings`.\n- **Webhooks:** `POST hook/<name>` turns any JSON (GitHub, Home Assistant, Grafana...) into a notification with `{{path}}` templates. Routes `GET`/`POST hooks`.\n- Panel: new tab \"Sources and rules\".\n- Tests for all of the above (`tests/notification-center-rules.test.cjs`).\n\n## 0.2.0\n- **Banner over the running app, no app switch** (default mode `banner`): the companion app's background service posts the native Samsung banner. Works with Vetro pairing on (the queue is handed over in a file).\n- Modes `banner`, `full` (full-screen card) and `both`; `duration` 1-60 s with a countdown; levels `info`, `ok`, `warn`, `alert`.\n- Emoji icons: by level or picked in the panel from a bundled Unicode list; the icon is cut at whole emoji only.\n- `image` and `wallpaper` on the full-screen card (fetched and served by the Shard).\n- ntfy-compatible route (`POST ntfy`) and a documented API: `API.md`, `GET api` (self-description), `GET status`.\n- Panel: three columns with a live preview, request generator, API explorer, installation status, English and Portuguese, recently sent list kept across restarts with Resend.\n- The full-screen app is relaunched as soon as it has left; a notification queued while it is open extends its stay; a restarted banner service never repeats a banner.\n- Node 4 compatibility: explicit request timeouts; run on a real Node 4.9.1.\n- Tests: Shard behaviour, the banner service (in a sandbox) and a check that `GET api`, `API.md`, this changelog and the manifest agree.\n\n## 0.1.0\n- First version: queue notifications over HTTP and show them as the native banner through a companion app that comes to the foreground.\n","README.md":"# Notification Center\n\n> **This Shard needs a companion app on the TV.** The app (an unsigned `.wgt`, id `NotifCtr01.NotifCenter`, with a background service\n> `NotifCtr01.Svc`) cannot be part of the catalog. Get it from https://github.com/mclaudiopt/tv-notification-center/releases, sign it with your own\n> certificate and install it (see \"Install\"). Without it, `notify` queues messages but nothing is shown.\n\nAny device on your LAN (PC, phone, script, another Shard, any ntfy client) can `POST` a message to this Shard. By default the TV shows it as a\n**native Samsung banner on top of whatever is running, without opening any app** (the companion app's background service posts it, and it also lands\nin the TV's own notification history). Optionally the message is shown as a **full-screen card**, which brings the companion app to the foreground for\na few seconds. Everything runs on the TV.\n\n**API for other apps and Shards:** [API.md](API.md) documents every field, route, limit and example, and `GET /plugins/notification-center/api/api`\nreturns the same description as JSON. Apps that already publish to [ntfy](https://ntfy.sh) can point at `POST .../api/ntfy` instead (headers `Title`,\n`Priority`, `Tags`).\n\n**Sources and rules** (tab in the panel, and the routes `profiles`, `settings`, `hooks` in [API.md](API.md)): each sender can have its own defaults, on/off switch,\nrate limit and key (`X-NC-Key`); quiet hours hold back everything below a chosen level; identical repeats are counted instead of shown again; and **webhooks**\n(`POST hook/<name>`) turn any JSON, such as a GitHub or Home Assistant payload, into a notification with `{{path}}` templates.\n\n**Sounds** (optional): a request can ask for one of the TV's system sounds with `sound` (and `volume`); the default is silence. This Shard plays it with one fixed `paplay` command (see Permissions), at most one sound every 0.8 s, with a volume you set next to the selector (the system sounds are quiet, so 100 % is already boosted), and in time with the banner (the sound starts a delay after the request that you tune by ear, because the Shard cannot see when the banner appears). The **Testing** tab has the sound tests, the timing, and a goodie: 10 seconds of a well-known tune played only with the notification sounds.\n\nThe panel (tab **Notifications**) is bilingual (English by default, Portuguese optional) and has a form, a request generator, an API explorer, an icon\npicker (emoji list bundled in `emoji.json`: Unicode CLDR data from [emojibase-data](https://github.com/milesj/emojibase), MIT, no network) and an\ninstallation status card.\n\n## Permissions\n\n`exec`, for **one fixed command** and only to play a sound: `paplay --volume=<integer> /usr/share/feedback/sound/operation/<file>`, where `<file>` is one of eight fixed names of the TV's own system sounds (`chime soft reward success alert rotation open poweron`) and the volume is a clamped integer. A request can only pick a name from that list: no text from a request ever reaches the command, and there is no other use of `exec` in the code (a test checks this). Sounds are never played unless a request asks for one.\n\n## Commands it runs\n\n| Command | Why |\n| --- | --- |\n| `paplay --volume=<integer> /usr/share/feedback/sound/operation/<file>.wav` | plays one of the TV's own system sounds when a notification asks for one (`sound`). `<file>` is one of 8 fixed names, the integer is 65536 times the volume you set (10-100 %) times the request's `volume` times 3 (the system sounds are quiet), and nothing else is ever run |\n\nIt runs only when a request has a `sound` (or calls `POST play`), at most once every 0.8 s, with an 8 second timeout. The goodie (`POST play` with `song`) runs the same command several times in a row from a fixed list in the code (10 seconds, one song at a time). It is the only use of `exec` in the code, and the tests check the exact shape of every command.\n## Network\n\n- **Incoming:** HTTP routes under `/plugins/notification-center/api/` (`notify`, `ntfy`, `notifications`, `img`, `recent`, `lang`, `emoji`, `status`,\n  `api`, `ui`, `nt.js`), open to every device that can reach Vetro (with pairing on, senders need a key).\n- **Outgoing, Vetro:** `POST 127.0.0.1:8099/apps/launch` with `{\"appId\": \"...\"}` to start the companion app or its service. The app id is fixed in the\n  code, so no route can point the Shard at another app. Launches of the app screen are limited to one every 2 seconds; the banner service is launched for every notification in banner mode (limit a noisy sender with its per-minute rate in \"Sources\").\n- **Outgoing, images:** no code is downloaded. The one exception is the optional `image` and `wallpaper` fields: the Shard fetches that URL once (GET, up\n  to 3 redirects, at most 2 downloads at once, 10 s in total each, at most 3 MB, only png/jpeg/gif/webp/bmp, kept in memory while queued) and serves it to the app from its own address, because the\n  app may only load origins it declares. Whoever can send notifications can therefore make the TV fetch an image URL, including one on your LAN (that is\n  how a doorbell snapshot works).\n- The companion app's service reads the queue from a file the Shard writes (`/tmp/notification-center-queue.json`), and falls back to the TV's own LAN\n  address, port 8099. Nothing leaves the TV.\n\n## Data it keeps\n\nIn `ctx.config` (on the TV, kept across restarts and updates):\n- the display language, and the list of the last 20 notifications sent (title, message, level, mode, icon, sound, image and wallpaper URLs, time), so \"Recently sent\" and Resend survive a restart;\n- the **sources** (profiles): per sender its icon, level, mode, duration, sound, volume, rate limit and on/off switch, and its **key in plain text** if it has one (the API never returns a key, only `hasKey`; but it is stored as text in the Shard's settings);\n- the **settings**: quiet hours, grouping window, strict mode, sound volume and sound delay;\n- the **webhooks** (their templates).\n\nThe queue (up to 5 active notifications) and the grouping memory (at most 200 recent notifications, only those inside the grouping window) live in memory and are cleared when the Shard stops.\nThe queue file and the file of already shown ids (`/tmp/notification-center-seen.json`) are temporary files in `/tmp`.\n\n**Grouping is on by default (60 s):** a notification identical to one sent in the last minute is counted instead of shown again (the answer says `merged`). The panel's Resend and test buttons send `group: false`, so they always show. Set `groupSeconds` to 0 to turn grouping off.\n## Install\n\n1. Vetro with root connected. If Vetro keeps restarting, set the TV's **Host PC IP to `127.0.0.1`** (Developer Mode), or it can end up in safe mode.\n2. Install this Shard from the catalog and switch it on.\n3. Install the companion app: download the unsigned `.wgt` from the releases linked above (or build it from the text files in [`app/`](app/) plus any\n   512x512 `icon.png`), sign it with your own certificate and install it with Apps2Samsung, TV-Store or Tizen Studio. It needs the privileges\n   `notification` and `internet`. The app id must stay `NotifCtr01.NotifCenter` and the service id `NotifCtr01.Svc`.\n4. Send a notification:\n\n```\ncurl -X POST http://<TV-IP>:8099/plugins/notification-center/api/notify \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"title\":\"Oven\",\"message\":\"Dinner is ready\",\"level\":\"ok\"}'\n```\n\nWith **pairing on** (Vetro's default) a request from another device needs `Authorization: Bearer <key>`: pair the script once (the TV shows a 6-digit\ncode) as described in Vetro's `SIGN-IN.md`. The companion app on the TV itself needs no key.\n\nIf the app shows \"nt.js failed\" (loopback does not answer on some TVs), build it with the TV's LAN address too: add it to `BASES` in `app/js/app.js`\nand `nt.js`, and a matching `<access origin=\"...\">` to `config.xml`.\n\n## How it works\n\n```\nmode \"banner\" (default, native banner over the running app, no app switch):\nany device --POST notify--> Shard (queue + queue file) --POST /apps/launch--> Vetro starts the app's background SERVICE\nservice --reads queue--> webapis.postPlainNotification(title, message, seconds) --> TV banner + notification history\n\nmodes \"full\" / \"both\" (full-screen card; the companion app comes to the foreground):\nany device --POST notify--> Shard (queue) --POST /apps/launch--> Vetro launches the companion app\napp --GET notifications--> Shard --> card on screen (+ native banner for \"both\") --> exits after `duration`\n```\n\nThe full-screen app is a thin loader: its logic is `nt.js`, served by this Shard, so it can change without reinstalling the app. The background service is\nself-contained.\n\n## Compatibility\n\n- **Vetro / Node:** the Shard is plain ES5 and uses only Node 4.4 APIs (request timeouts are set with `setTimeout`, because the `timeout` option only exists\n  from Node 6.8). It was checked with an ES5-only parser, and the runtime lifecycle test (`npm run test:runtime`) plus a request/queue/icon test pass on a **real\n  Node 4.9.1** (the last 4.x release). The Node 4.4.7 binary itself crashes at start on the Windows 11 machine used for the work, so 4.4.7 exactly has not been run.\n- **Samsung TVs:** the app declares `required_version=\"6.0\"`, so it needs **Tizen 6.0 or newer (2021 models and later)**. Everything was tested on one TV\n  only: **Samsung Q70C, Tizen 9.0, Vetro 0.7.0**. Older TVs and Smart Monitors have not been tried.\n\n## Limitations (all measured on a real TV)\n\n- The native banner (`webapis.postPlainNotification`, which exists only inside a web *service*) takes plain text and a duration: **no icon, level or colour**.\n  The Shard puts an emoji in front of the title instead (by level, or the icon you choose).\n- Full-screen card / both: the companion app comes to the front, shows the card for `duration` seconds and exits, so what was playing is interrupted briefly.\n  These modes still read the Shard over the network, so they need Vetro pairing off (or a TV where loopback answers). Banner mode works with pairing on.\n- The service cannot reach Vetro over loopback on the TV tested (`EHOSTUNREACH`), which is why the queue is handed over as a file.\n- The TV refuses to run unsigned script files; this Shard needs none.\n- Native notification routes other than the web service (`notification_test`, `libnotification` sockets, D-Bus, writing the notification database) were dead\n  ends; see the repository's `docs/NATIVE-NOTIFICATIONS.md`.\n- Whether the previous screen returns cleanly was tested only from the launcher, not over every source (live TV, HDMI).\n- With pairing off, any device that can reach Vetro's port can send notifications and make Vetro launch the companion app.\n\n## Tested on\n\nSamsung Q70C (TQ55Q70CATXXC), Tizen 9.0, Vetro 0.7.0, Shard 0.2.0 installed from the bundle as a file (Developer mode), companion app 0.2.2.\n\n- **Pairing off:** notifications sent with `curl` from a PC: native banner (`banner`), full-screen card (`full`) and both; English and Portuguese; the four\n  levels with their icons; `duration`; Resend from \"Recently sent\"; the TV returned to the previous app each time.\n- **Pairing on (Vetro 0.7.0 \"Require pairing\"):** banner mode tested (a notification sent with a Bearer key appeared as a native banner over the running\n  app; an unpaired request got `401`). Full-screen modes not tested with pairing on.\n- **Not tested:** live TV and HDMI as the \"previous screen\", other TV models, other Tizen versions.\n\nChanges are listed in [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md).\n","app/config.xml":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<widget xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/ns/widgets\" xmlns:tizen=\"http://tizen.org/ns/widgets\" id=\"http://localhost/notifcenter\" version=\"0.2.2\" viewmodes=\"maximized\">\n  <tizen:application id=\"NotifCtr01.NotifCenter\" package=\"NotifCtr01\" required_version=\"6.0\"/>\n  <name>Notification Center</name>\n  <icon src=\"icon.png\"/>\n  <content src=\"index.html\"/>\n  <access origin=\"http://127.0.0.1:8099\" subdomains=\"false\"/>\n  <feature name=\"http://tizen.org/feature/screen.size.all\"/>\n  <tizen:privilege name=\"http://tizen.org/privilege/internet\"/>\n  <tizen:privilege name=\"http://tizen.org/privilege/notification\"/>\n  <tizen:profile name=\"tv-samsung\"/>\n  <tizen:service id=\"NotifCtr01.Svc\" auto-restart=\"false\" on-boot=\"false\">\n    <tizen:content src=\"service.js\"/>\n    <tizen:name>Notification Center service</tizen:name>\n    <tizen:icon src=\"icon.png\"/>\n    <tizen:description>Shows the Samsung notification banner without opening an app</tizen:description>\n  </tizen:service>\n</widget>\n","app/index.html":"<!doctype html>\n<html><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><title>Notification Center</title></head>\n<body style=\"background:#0f1218;color:#e8ecf2;font:28px sans-serif;margin:40px\">\n<div id=\"log\"></div>\n<script src=\"js/app.js\"></script>\n</body></html>\n","app/js/app.js":"(function () {\n  \"use strict\";\n  var BASES = [\"http://127.0.0.1:8099\"];\n  var PATH = \"/plugins/notification-center/api/nt.js\";\n  var logEl = document.getElementById(\"log\"), i = 0;\n  function say(t) { var d = document.createElement(\"div\"); d.textContent = t; logEl.appendChild(d); }\n  (function next() {\n    if (i >= BASES.length) { say(\"Could not load nt.js from the Shard. Is it running?\"); return; }\n    var s = document.createElement(\"script\");\n    s.src = BASES[i++] + PATH + \"?t=\" + new Date().getTime();\n    s.onerror = function () { say(\"nt.js failed: \" + s.src.split(\"?\")[0]); next(); };\n    (document.head || document.documentElement).appendChild(s);\n  })();\n})();\n","app/service.js":"// Background service of the Notification Center companion app (Node.js in the TV's web service runtime).\n// Vetro starts it (POST /apps/launch) each time a notification is queued in \"banner\" mode; it posts the native Samsung banner with\n// webapis.postPlainNotification, without opening any app.\n// It reads the queue from a file the Shard writes (no network, no Vetro key: it works with pairing on). If that file is missing it asks the\n// Shard over the network, on the TV's own LAN address (loopback is not reachable from the service on every TV).\n// The ids it has already shown are kept in a file of its own, so a restarted service never shows the same banner twice.\nvar QUEUE_FILE = '/tmp/notification-center-queue.json';\nvar SEEN_FILE = '/tmp/notification-center-seen.json';\nvar HOSTS = ['127.0.0.1'];\ntry {\n  var ifs = require('os').networkInterfaces();\n  Object.keys(ifs).forEach(function (k) { ifs[k].forEach(function (i) { if (i.family === 'IPv4' && !i.internal && HOSTS.indexOf(i.address) < 0) { HOSTS.push(i.address); } }); });\n} catch (e) { /* keep loopback only */ }\nvar PATH = '/plugins/notification-center/api/notifications';\nvar EMOJI = { info: '\\u2139\\uFE0F', ok: '\\u2705', warn: '\\u26A0\\uFE0F', alert: '\\uD83D\\uDD34' };\nvar seen = {};\ntry { seen = JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync(SEEN_FILE, 'utf8')) || {}; } catch (e) { seen = {}; }\n\nfunction saveSeen() {\n  try {\n    var keys = Object.keys(seen);\n    if (keys.length > 60) { keys.slice(0, keys.length - 60).forEach(function (k) { delete seen[k]; }); }\n    require('fs').writeFileSync(SEEN_FILE, JSON.stringify(seen));\n  } catch (e) { /* not writable: duplicates are possible only if the service restarts while a notification is still queued */ }\n}\nfunction show(n) {\n  // The native banner takes plain text only, but it draws emoji: the icon (or the level's emoji) goes in front of the title.\n  var head = n.icon || EMOJI[n.level] || '';\n  var title = (head ? head + ' ' : '') + (n.title || 'Notification');\n  try { webapis.postPlainNotification(title, n.message || '', parseInt(n.duration, 10) || 8); } catch (e) { /* API not available */ }\n}\nfunction handle(list) {\n  var changed = false;\n  list.forEach(function (n) {\n    if (seen[n.id]) { return; }\n    seen[n.id] = 1; changed = true;\n    if (n.mode !== 'banner') { return; }                          // \"full\" and \"both\" are shown by the companion app screen\n    if (n.expires && n.expires < Date.now()) { return; }          // too old to be worth showing\n    show(n);\n  });\n  if (changed) { saveSeen(); }\n}\nfunction fromNetwork() {\n  var i = 0;\n  (function next() {\n    if (i >= HOSTS.length) { return; }\n    fetch('http://' + HOSTS[i++] + ':8099' + PATH + '?t=' + Date.now()).then(function (r) { if (!r.ok) { throw new Error('http ' + r.status); } return r.json(); }).then(function (d) {\n      handle(d.notifications || []);\n    }).catch(function () { next(); });\n  })();\n}\nfunction check() {\n  var d = null;\n  try { d = JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync(QUEUE_FILE, 'utf8')); } catch (e) { d = null; }\n  if (d && Array.isArray(d.notifications)) { handle(d.notifications); } else { fromNetwork(); }\n}\nmodule.exports.onStart = check;\nmodule.exports.onRequest = check;\nmodule.exports.onStop = function () {};\n","emoji.json":"{\"source\":\"Unicode CLDR via emojibase-data 18.0.0 (MIT)\",\"groups\":[\"Smileys\",\"People\",\"Animals\",\"Food\",\"Travel\",\"Activities\",\"Objects\",\"Symbols\",\"Flags\"],\"list\":[[\"\\ud83d\\ude00\",0,\"grinning face\",\"rosto risonho\",\"cheerful cheery face grin\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude03\",0,\"grinning face with big eyes\",\"rosto risonho com olhos bem abertos\",\"awesome big eyes face\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude04\",0,\"grinning face with smiling eyes\",\"rosto risonho com olhos sorridentes\",\"eye eyes face grin\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude01\",0,\"beaming face with smiling eyes\",\"rosto contente com olhos sorridentes\",\"beaming eye eyes face\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude06\",0,\"grinning squinting face\",\"rosto risonho com olhos semicerrados\",\"closed eyes face grinning\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude05\",0,\"grinning face with sweat\",\"rosto risonho com gota de suor\",\"cold dejected excited face\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd23\",0,\"rolling on the floor laughing\",\"rolando no ch\\u00e3o de rir\",\"crying face floor funny\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude02\",0,\"face with tears of joy\",\"rosto chorando de rir\",\"crying face feels funny\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude42\",0,\"slightly smiling face\",\"rosto levemente sorridente\",\"face happy slightly smile\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude43\",0,\"upside-down face\",\"rosto de cabe\\u00e7a para baixo\",\"face hehe smile upside-down\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude09\",0,\"winking face\",\"rosto com olho piscando\",\"face flirt heartbreaker sexy\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude0a\",0,\"smiling face with smiling eyes\",\"rosto sorridente com olhos sorridentes\",\"blush eye eyes face\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude07\",0,\"smiling face with halo\",\"rosto sorridente com aur\\u00e9ola\",\"angel angelic angels blessed\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd70\",0,\"smiling face with hearts\",\"rosto sorridente com 3 cora\\u00e7\\u00f5es\",\"3 adore crush face\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude0d\",0,\"smiling face with heart-eyes\",\"rosto sorridente com olhos de cora\\u00e7\\u00e3o\",\"143 bae eye face\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd29\",0,\"star-struck\",\"rosto com olhar maravilhado\",\"excited eyes face grinning\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude18\",0,\"face blowing a kiss\",\"rosto mandando um beijo\",\"adorbs bae blowing face\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude17\",0,\"kissing face\",\"rosto beijando\",\"143 date dating face\"],[\"\\u263a\\ufe0f\",0,\"smiling face\",\"rosto sorridente\",\"face happy outlined relaxed\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude1a\",0,\"kissing face with closed eyes\",\"rosto beijando com olhos fechados\",\"143 bae blush closed\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude19\",0,\"kissing face with smiling eyes\",\"rosto beijando com olhos sorridentes\",\"143 closed date dating\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd72\",0,\"smiling face with tear\",\"rosto sorridente com l\\u00e1grima\",\"face glad grateful happy\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude0b\",0,\"face savoring food\",\"rosto saboreando comida\",\"delicious eat face food\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude1b\",0,\"face with tongue\",\"rosto mostrando a l\\u00edngua\",\"awesome cool face nice\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude1c\",0,\"winking face with tongue\",\"rosto piscando e com l\\u00edngua para fora\",\"crazy epic eye face\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd2a\",0,\"zany face\",\"rosto bizarro\",\"crazy eye eyes face\"],[\"\\ud83d\\ude1d\",0,\"squinting face with tongue\",\"rosto com olhos semicerrados e l\\u00edngua para fora\",\"closed eye eyes face\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd11\",0,\"money-mouth face\",\"rosto com cifr\\u00f5es\",\"face money money-mouth mouth\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd17\",0,\"smiling face with open hands\",\"rosto abra\\u00e7ando\",\"face hands hug hugging\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd2d\",0,\"face with hand over mouth\",\"rosto com a m\\u00e3o sobre a boca\",\"face giggle giggling hand\"],[\"\\ud83e\\udd2b\",0,\"shushing face\",\"rosto fazendo sinal de sil\\u00eancio\",\"face quiet shh shu